  <div class="blue-debrief-card">
    <h3>SOAR Best Practices</h3>
    <p><strong>The enrichment-first pattern:</strong> Every effective SOAR playbook starts by gathering context. Enrichment steps (reputation checks, WHOIS lookups, sandbox analysis) transform a raw alert into an informed decision. Without enrichment, you're guessing.</p>
    <p><strong>Decision layering:</strong> No single indicator is reliable enough to act on alone. Combine sender reputation + URL analysis + domain age for a confidence score. A new domain (< 30 days) with a low sender reputation and a blocklisted URL is almost certainly malicious. Any one of those alone might be a false positive.</p>
    <p><strong>The automation spectrum:</strong> Not everything should be fully automated. Use a tiered approach: auto-close obvious false positives, auto-quarantine obvious threats, and route edge cases to analysts. The goal is to reduce analyst workload by 60-80%, not to replace them entirely.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="blue-debrief-card">
    <h3>Enrichment Sources</h3>
    <p><strong>VirusTotal (sender reputation):</strong> Checks the sender's email address and IP against 70+ antivirus engines and threat intelligence feeds. A score above 70 means multiple engines flagged this sender as malicious.</p>
    <p><strong>URLhaus (URL reputation):</strong> A project by Abuse.ch that tracks malware distribution URLs. If a URL in the email appears on this blocklist, it's distributing malware or is a phishing page.</p>
    <p><strong>WHOIS (domain age):</strong> Newly registered domains (< 30 days old) are a strong phishing indicator. Attackers register domains, use them for a campaign, and abandon them. Legitimate businesses have domains that are years old.</p>
    <p><strong>Other sources to consider:</strong> Sandbox analysis (detonate attachments), email header analysis (SPF/DKIM/DMARC failures), and internal threat intelligence (has this sender targeted us before?).</p>
  </div>
  <div class="blue-debrief-card">
    <h3>Decision Thresholds</h3>
    <p><strong>Tuning is iterative.</strong> Start with conservative thresholds (fewer auto-actions, more analyst review) and adjust based on false positive/negative rates. Review playbook performance weekly for the first month.</p>
    <p><strong>Sender reputation > 70:</strong> This threshold catches most known malicious senders while minimizing false positives. Lower it to 50 to be more aggressive (catches more, but more false positives). Raise it to 90 for high-confidence only.</p>
    <p><strong>Domain age < 30 days:</strong> Most phishing domains are used within days of registration. This threshold catches the majority of phishing campaigns. Some legitimate new businesses will trigger this — route to analysts rather than auto-blocking.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="blue-debrief-card">
    <h3>Automation ROI</h3>
    <p>With 200 phishing alerts per week and 15 minutes per alert, your SOC team spends <strong>50 hours per week</strong> on phishing triage alone.</p>
    <p>A well-tuned SOAR playbook with 80% accuracy can auto-handle 160 of those 200 alerts, reducing manual triage to <strong>10 hours per week</strong> — a 40-hour savings.</p>
    <p>At an average analyst salary, that's approximately <strong>$100,000/year in recovered productivity</strong> — time analysts can spend on threat hunting, detection engineering, and incident response instead of repetitive triage.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="blue-debrief-card">
    <h3>Iterating on Playbooks</h3>
    <p><strong>Track metrics:</strong> Monitor true positive rate, false positive rate, mean time to respond (MTTR), and analyst override rate. If analysts are overriding the playbook frequently, your thresholds need tuning.</p>
    <p><strong>Feedback loops:</strong> When an analyst overrides a playbook decision, capture why. Use those overrides to refine decision logic and add new enrichment sources.</p>
    <p><strong>Version control:</strong> Treat playbooks like code. Version them, review changes, and test in staging before production. A bad playbook update can auto-close real threats or quarantine legitimate emails at scale.</p>
    <p><strong>Continuous enrichment:</strong> As new threat intelligence feeds become available, add them as enrichment steps. The more context your playbook has, the better its decisions.</p>
  </div>
</div>

